<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("world_size") %>
<% sides = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("world_size").map { |arm| arm.fetch("width") } %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 2 sets two readings of emergence against each other, and they predict
  opposite things about a bigger world. If a replicator is a lottery win — one cell
  somewhere stumbles into a tape that copies itself, and the rest of the world only has to
  not destroy it — then cells are tickets: quadrupling the cell count should cut the
  expected time to the first transition by about the same factor, and the smallest worlds
  should mostly never transition inside the budget. If instead emergence is a
  <em>per-cell rate</em> — each cell needs its own local history of interactions before
  anything can replicate — then the transition epoch hardly moves with cell count, and the
  arms land on top of each other.
</p>
<p>
  The two readings differ in what a world is for. Under the lottery reading the world is a
  parallel search and its size is the search budget; under the rate reading the world is a
  population of independent local experiments, each running at its own speed.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Square tori of 64-byte tapes at
  <span class="mono"><%= sides.map { |side| "#{side}×#{side}" }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  — width and height travel together, so the arms are
  <%= sides.map { |side| number_with_delimiter(side * side) }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %>
  cells —
  with <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds each and <%= epochs %> epochs per run. Every
  other parameter is at its default. The dependent variable is the transition epoch of
  DESIGN §1.2, the first sampled epoch at which
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls below 0.6 and stays below it for three
  more samples; a run that never crosses is censored, not zero.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  Partial. All but two runs of the sweep are terminal, and the shape is already plain, but
  the two that are still running are in the largest arm — the only arm where anything has
  happened. What follows states the shape; the per-run epochs and counts are in the phase
  diagram and the evidence tables below, which read the runs themselves.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Replicators appear only in the largest world.</strong> At this sweep's mutation
  rate — the engine default, twice the rate at which the
  <%= link_to "mutation-rate sweep", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> first saw
  emergence — no seed of the two smallest worlds moved on either observable. In the largest
  world three seeds moved on both: the transition detector flagged them and the
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census counted a colony in each, one of them
  the largest census the lab has recorded. The two seeds still running are in that arm, so
  its fraction can only rise.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Below it, a collapse that is not a replicator.</strong> One seed of the
  second-largest world collapses <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> from the random-soup
  value to under 5, drives <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> to a fraction of the soup
  floor and lifts <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> off zero — with a census of zero at
  every sample. It is the disagreement class the mutation-rate sweep also reports: a
  low-entropy attractor the substrate can fall into that is not a replicator by the test
  DESIGN §1.2 locks. Read with the census, the smaller worlds are not slow versions of the
  largest one; they are doing something else.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The two sweeps do not agree yet.</strong> The mutation-rate sweep found a
  replicator at the width of this sweep's second-largest arm, at half this sweep's mutation
  rate, while every seed of that arm here found none. The two differ in nothing else. So
  either the rate matters more at that width than the cell count does, or emergence there is
  rare enough that one event in ten seeds and none in ten seeds are the same underlying rate
  seen twice. <%= link_to "Mutation rate, long runs", experiment_path("mutation-rate-long") %>
  is the sweep that separates those.
</p>

<p>
  Two things keep this partial rather than published, and both are about censoring. A run
  that never crosses is censored, not negative: the <%= epochs %>-epoch budget is short next
  to the epochs at which the transitions here actually landed, so an arm that shows nothing
  may be slower rather than silent, and the two unfinished seeds of the largest arm are not
  a zero either. And a sweep of ours is only readable as a negative once the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> resolves its own
  census; until then an arm sitting at the censoring line says as much about the instrument
  as about the world.
</p>
